## Exemplary Patent-Style Draft

### Title

**Systems and Methods for Controlling Oxygenated Secondary-Fuel Injection in a Diesel Internal Combustion Engine**

### Abstract

A fuel-control system for a compression-ignition internal combustion engine controls introduction of an oxygenated secondary fuel in conjunction with a diesel primary fuel. A controller determines an operating state of the engine using sensor data, operator inputs, signals from an engine control module, and road-topology information. The controller selectively enables, disables, and modulates delivery of the oxygenated secondary fuel based on the operating state, predicted engine loading, combustion constraints, emissions constraints, and one or more efficiency criteria. The controller may determine a target secondary-fuel substitution ratio, injection timing, injection duration, flow rate, pressure, or distribution among cylinders, and may override or derate secondary-fuel delivery in response to transient operation, detected faults, or operation outside a permitted control envelope.

---

## Description

### Technical Field

The present disclosure relates generally to fuel-control systems for compression-ignition engines and, more particularly, to systems and methods for controlling injection of an oxygenated secondary fuel into a diesel internal combustion engine.

### System Overview

In one embodiment, an engine system includes a compression-ignition internal combustion engine configured to receive a diesel primary fuel and an oxygenated secondary fuel. The oxygenated secondary fuel may include, without limitation, an alcohol, an ether, an oxygenated hydrocarbon, or a mixture thereof. Examples include methanol, ethanol, dimethyl ether, and combinations containing one or more oxygenated fuel constituents.

A secondary-fuel delivery subsystem may include a storage vessel, pump, pressure regulator, filter, conduit, one or more control valves, one or more injectors, and associated pressure and temperature sensors. The oxygenated secondary fuel may be introduced into an intake passage, intake manifold, charge-air passage, individual cylinder intake port, combustion chamber, or another location upstream of or within one or more cylinders.

A secondary-fuel controller is operatively coupled to the secondary-fuel delivery subsystem and may communicate with an engine control module over a vehicle communication network. The secondary-fuel controller may be physically separate from, integrated with, or implemented as a functional portion of the engine control module.

### Control Inputs

The controller may receive or derive one or more of:

- engine speed;
- engine torque, requested torque, or estimated load;
- diesel-fuel quantity, rail pressure, and injection timing;
- intake-manifold pressure and temperature;
- mass airflow;
- charge-air temperature and pressure;
- exhaust temperature;
- exhaust oxygen concentration;
- nitrogen-oxide concentration;
- particulate concentration or filter loading;
- combustion pressure, knock, misfire, or combustion-phasing information;
- coolant, lubricant, fuel, or catalyst temperature;
- vehicle speed and acceleration;
- transmission gear or shift state;
- accelerator-pedal position;
- brake-pedal position;
- cruise-control status;
- operator-selected operating mode;
- secondary-fuel level, pressure, temperature, composition, or quality;
- ambient temperature, pressure, humidity, or altitude;
- traction-control or stability-control status; and
- diagnostic or fault information.

Operator inputs may include an enable command, disable command, economy-mode selection, performance-mode selection, emissions-mode selection, maximum substitution setting, minimum reserve setting, or destination and route information.

Signals from the engine control module may include actual or requested torque, diesel-injection quantity, diesel-injection timing, engine-governor state, idle state, regeneration state, exhaust-brake state, cold-start state, thermal-management request, derate state, and diagnostic information.

### Road-Topology Inputs

Road-topology information may be obtained from a navigation system, digital map, global-navigation-satellite-system receiver, vehicle-to-infrastructure communication device, camera, radar, lidar, fleet-management system, stored route model, or combinations thereof.

The road-topology information may identify a current or upcoming road grade, grade length, curvature, speed limit, intersection, stop, traffic condition, elevation change, construction zone, geofenced emissions area, or predicted deceleration event. The controller may use such information to predict engine loading over a future time or travel-distance horizon.

For example, the controller may increase or otherwise prepare secondary-fuel delivery in advance of an uphill grade, reduce secondary-fuel delivery before a predicted low-load or deceleration interval, or reserve secondary fuel for a route segment in which use of the secondary fuel is predicted to provide a greater efficiency or emissions benefit.

### Efficiency-Based Control

The controller may evaluate an efficiency objective using a cost function or optimization routine. The efficiency objective may account for one or more of:

- brake thermal efficiency;
- diesel displacement or substitution;
- total fuel energy consumption;
- fuel cost;
- carbon dioxide emissions;
- nitrogen-oxide emissions;
- particulate emissions;
- exhaust-aftertreatment temperature;
- secondary-fuel availability;
- route completion requirements;
- drivability;
- engine durability; and
- compliance with combustion and emissions limits.

The controller may compare predicted operation with and without secondary-fuel injection and authorize injection when a predicted benefit exceeds a threshold. The threshold may be fixed or may vary based on route, fuel inventory, operator preference, engine condition, ambient condition, or applicable emissions requirements.

The commanded secondary-fuel amount may be expressed as a mass flow, energy flow, volumetric flow, injection pulse width, duty cycle, or substitution ratio. The substitution ratio may represent a fraction of total fuel mass, total fuel volume, or total supplied fuel energy attributable to the oxygenated secondary fuel.

### Protective and Override Functions

Secondary-fuel delivery may be reduced or inhibited during starting, warm-up, idle, rapid torque transients, gear shifting, traction-control intervention, exhaust braking, diesel particulate-filter regeneration, excessive exhaust temperature, excessive cylinder pressure, unstable combustion, misfire, knock, low secondary-fuel pressure, loss of sensor validity, communications failure, or another condition outside a permitted operating envelope.

A fallback mode may discontinue secondary-fuel delivery while permitting continued operation on diesel fuel. The controller may close redundant shutoff valves, purge a delivery passage, store a diagnostic code, and notify an operator upon detection of a fault.

---

## Exemplary Claims

### 1. Independent System Claim

1. **A fuel-control system for a compression-ignition internal combustion engine, the fuel-control system comprising:**

   a secondary-fuel delivery subsystem configured to introduce an oxygenated secondary fuel into an airflow path or a combustion chamber of the compression-ignition internal combustion engine, the engine being further configured to receive a diesel primary fuel;

   at least one communication interface configured to receive:

   (i) sensor data indicative of an operating condition of the engine or a vehicle carrying the engine,  
   (ii) an operator input,  
   (iii) an engine-controller signal, and  
   (iv) road-topology information indicative of a current or upcoming road condition; and

   at least one controller configured to:

   determine a current operating state of the engine from at least a portion of the sensor data, the operator input, and the engine-controller signal;

   predict a future loading condition of the engine based at least in part on the road-topology information;

   determine, according to an efficiency criterion and at least one combustion or emissions constraint, a target delivery parameter for the oxygenated secondary fuel; and

   control the secondary-fuel delivery subsystem according to the target delivery parameter.

### 2. Dependent Claims

2. The fuel-control system of claim 1, wherein the oxygenated secondary fuel comprises an alcohol, an ether, or a combination thereof.

3. The fuel-control system of claim 1, wherein the target delivery parameter comprises at least one of a secondary-fuel mass flow, a secondary-fuel energy flow, an injection pressure, an injection timing, an injection duration, or a secondary-fuel substitution ratio.

4. The fuel-control system of claim 1, wherein the sensor data includes at least one of engine speed, engine load, intake-manifold pressure, mass airflow, exhaust temperature, exhaust oxygen concentration, nitrogen-oxide concentration, particulate concentration, cylinder pressure, secondary-fuel pressure, or secondary-fuel temperature.

5. The fuel-control system of claim 1, wherein the operator input comprises at least one of a secondary-fuel enable command, an operating-mode selection, a maximum-substitution setting, a fuel-reserve setting, or route information.

6. The fuel-control system of claim 1, wherein the engine-controller signal comprises at least one of requested torque, actual torque, diesel-injection quantity, diesel-injection timing, transmission state, regeneration state, thermal-management state, or diagnostic status.

7. The fuel-control system of claim 1, wherein the road-topology information identifies at least one of road grade, elevation, grade length, road curvature, speed limit, intersection location, traffic condition, or predicted vehicle deceleration.

8. The fuel-control system of claim 1, wherein the controller is configured to increase, initiate, or precondition delivery of the oxygenated secondary fuel before the vehicle reaches an uphill road segment.

9. The fuel-control system of claim 1, wherein the controller is configured to reduce or discontinue delivery of the oxygenated secondary fuel before a predicted deceleration, downhill segment, stop, or low-load interval.

10. The fuel-control system of claim 1, wherein the efficiency criterion comprises at least one of brake thermal efficiency, diesel-fuel displacement, total fuel-energy consumption, fuel cost, carbon-dioxide output, or availability of the oxygenated secondary fuel.

11. The fuel-control system of claim 1, wherein the controller is configured to determine the target delivery parameter by minimizing a cost function that includes a fuel-consumption term and at least one of an emissions term, a drivability term, a thermal-limit term, or a secondary-fuel inventory term.

12. The fuel-control system of claim 1, wherein the controller is configured to estimate an efficiency benefit associated with delivery of the oxygenated secondary fuel and to inhibit such delivery when the estimated efficiency benefit fails to satisfy a threshold.

13. The fuel-control system of claim 1, wherein the controller is configured to allocate a finite quantity of the oxygenated secondary fuel among a plurality of predicted route segments according to respective predicted efficiency benefits.

14. The fuel-control system of claim 1, wherein the controller is configured to modify diesel-primary-fuel injection in coordination with delivery of the oxygenated secondary fuel so as to maintain a requested engine torque.

15. The fuel-control system of claim 1, wherein the controller is configured to limit delivery of the oxygenated secondary fuel based on a limit associated with cylinder pressure, pressure-rise rate, combustion stability, exhaust temperature, nitrogen-oxide output, particulate output, or aftertreatment operation.

16. The fuel-control system of claim 1, wherein the controller is configured to disable delivery of the oxygenated secondary fuel in response to a sensor fault, a communication fault, insufficient secondary-fuel pressure, detected leakage, misfire, knock, or operation outside a predetermined engine-speed-and-load region.

17. The fuel-control system of claim 1, wherein the secondary-fuel delivery subsystem comprises a plurality of injectors independently controllable to vary delivery of the oxygenated secondary fuel among respective cylinders.

18. The fuel-control system of claim 1, wherein the road-topology information is received from at least one of a navigation system, a digital map, a global-navigation-satellite-system receiver, a vehicle-to-infrastructure interface, or a forward-looking vehicle sensor.

### 19. Independent Method Claim

19. **A method of controlling fueling of a compression-ignition internal combustion engine, the method comprising:**

   supplying diesel fuel to the engine;

   receiving sensor data indicative of engine or vehicle operation;

   receiving an operator input and a signal from an engine controller;

   obtaining road-topology information for a route traveled or expected to be traveled by a vehicle carrying the engine;

   determining a present engine operating state;

   predicting a future engine-load condition using the road-topology information;

   determining a commanded quantity of an oxygenated secondary fuel based on the present engine operating state, the predicted future engine-load condition, an efficiency objective, and at least one operating constraint; and

   selectively introducing the commanded quantity of the oxygenated secondary fuel into an intake flow or a combustion chamber of the engine.

20. The method of claim 19, further comprising reducing a commanded diesel-fuel quantity in relation to an energy contribution of the oxygenated secondary fuel.

21. The method of claim 19, further comprising updating the commanded quantity in closed-loop fashion using measured exhaust composition, combustion pressure, engine torque, or engine speed.

22. The method of claim 19, further comprising predicting an efficiency benefit for each of a plurality of upcoming route segments and preferentially consuming the oxygenated secondary fuel during a route segment having a greater predicted efficiency benefit.

23. The method of claim 19, further comprising overriding the commanded quantity in response to a safety limit, emissions limit, aftertreatment request, transient torque condition, or detected fault.

24. The method of claim 19, wherein determining the commanded quantity includes selecting a substitution ratio that maximizes predicted fuel-conversion efficiency while maintaining cylinder pressure, combustion stability, and exhaust temperature within respective limits.

### 25. Computer-Readable-Medium Claim

25. **A non-transitory computer-readable medium** storing instructions that, when executed by one or more processors associated with a compression-ignition engine, cause the one or more processors to:

   receive engine sensor data, an operator input, an engine-controller signal, and road-topology data;

   estimate a current engine operating state and a future engine loading profile;

   determine, using an efficiency-based control objective, a target amount of an oxygenated secondary fuel to be supplied in combination with diesel fuel;

   enforce at least one combustion, emissions, thermal, or safety constraint; and

   generate a command for a secondary-fuel injector or flow-control device according to the target amount.
